# ── CORRECTION, 2026-08-25 15:10 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The line above labels the 3,792,749 discarded blocks "all-negative (genus>=2 only)". That label is
# WRONG and the number is right. Those blocks produce NOTHING, at any genus.
#
# Every finite point set has a vertex on its convex hull; at a hull vertex every incident edge points
# into one half-space, so the vertex figure lies in a hemisphere and the face angles sum under 360.
# So EVERY closed polyhedron in R^3 — any genus, embedded or self-intersecting — has at least one
# CONVEX vertex, and a block whose every vertex type is a saddle cannot be realized at all.
#
# Tested rather than assumed: 400 all-negative blocks sampled from the unfiltered pruned set, blocks
# the developer had never seen because this filter discarded them. ZERO realized, and they fail in the
# flood fill, which is where a surface that does not exist should fail.
#
# Consequence for this run, and it is the useful part: the filter was built to hunt GENUS 1, where
# Gauss-Bonnet forces total defect zero and therefore both signs. For genus >= 2 curvature alone
# permits an all-saddle solid, so on the reasoning available at the time these 3.79M blocks should
# have been kept and searched. The theorem says they were dead. The 21,121 that survived are therefore
# the exhaustive candidate set for EVERY genus at k=2, not just for tori.
#
# ⚑ That is a statement about the CANDIDATE SET and not about the shelf. develop's guard is still
# max(2000, develop_spherical.instance_bound), and instance_bound's derivation assumes a rotation axis
# meets the surface at a vertex, an edge midpoint or a face centre — false when the axis runs down a
# hole. So the search is exhaustive and the develop stage is capped, and "complete" needs both.

# ── RETRACTION OF THE CORRECTION ABOVE, 2026-08-25 ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The correction block above is WRONG and is retracted. It claimed every closed polyhedron in R^3 has
# at least one CONVEX vertex, proved it from the convex hull, and concluded that all-saddle blocks are
# dead and 96.6% of a k=3 search could be discarded. All three parts are false.
#
# THE COUNTEREXAMPLE IS ALREADY IN THIS ATLAS. ss-12-30-12-d3, the great dodecahedron: twelve
# vertices, five pentagons at each, 5*108 = 540 degrees. Every vertex a saddle, not one convex corner.
# Ten star-shelf solids are all-saddle — ditrigonal dodecadodecahedron, great ditrigonal
# icosidodecahedron, snub icosidodecadodecahedron and four unnamed ones with them.
#
# THE PROOF'S ERROR. "The farthest vertex in any direction has all its neighbours behind it, so its
# vertex figure lies in a hemisphere, so its angles sum under 360" holds only when the vertex figure is
# a SIMPLE closed curve. A self-intersecting solid's vertex figure can cross itself, and a
# self-crossing spherical polygon inside a hemisphere has no perimeter bound. Measured over the 456
# shipped Polyhedron records: 59 of them have a SADDLE as their farthest vertex in some direction.
#
# THE EVIDENCE'S ERROR, which is the worse one. The 400-block test that "confirmed" it drew every
# block from a k=2 run over a six-tile CONVEX palette. Each counterexample carries a star face, which
# that palette does not contain. The claim was tested on exactly the population where it holds.
#
# ⚑ A THIRD ERROR, caught only by re-measuring: the first corpus scan computed interior angles as
# 180 - 360/n from the RING LENGTH, which reads a {5/2} as a pentagon (108 instead of 36). Angles must
# come from the coordinates, or from faceType, never from the ring length.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES. The k=2 filter is still right, for the narrow reason it was built on and nothing more:
# genus 1 forces total defect to zero, which needs both signs. That is Gauss-Bonnet and it stands. The
# claim that the same filter was also complete for genus >= 2 does NOT stand — 3,792,749 all-saddle
# blocks were discarded unexamined and may hold higher-genus solids.
#
# STILL OPEN, and being tested rather than asserted this time: whether an all-saddle solid can exist
# over the CONVEX palette {3,4,5,6,8,10}, where every known counterexample's star face is unavailable.
# Testing it by looking where a counterexample would hide, not by sampling again: the great
# dodecahedron is vertex-transitive, so the 2,839 all-saddle k=1 blocks are the highest-yield place,
# and every one of them was discarded by the genus-1 filter without ever being developed.
# See k1-saddle-2026-08-25.log.
